# {{.ProviderShortName}} Provider

{{ .Description | trimspace }}

## Advanced: Custom Headers

Most users do not need `custom_headers`. This option is intended for restricted self-hosted Logfire deployments or environments where requests must pass through a proxy, gateway, or edge authentication layer before reaching Logfire.

For example:

```terraform
provider "logfire" {
  api_key  = var.logfire_api_key
  base_url = var.logfire_base_url

  custom_headers = {
    "CF-Access-Client-Id"     = var.cloudflare_access_client_id
    "CF-Access-Client-Secret" = var.cloudflare_access_client_secret
  }
}
```

Header values should be supplied from Terraform variables or another secret source.

The Logfire `api_key` remains the provider's API authentication mechanism. `custom_headers` only configures extra transport headers.

The provider rejects these header names case-insensitively because they are managed by the provider or API client:

- `Authorization`
- `Content-Type`
- `Accept`
- `User-Agent`
- `X-Terraform-Provider-Version`

## Self-hosted compatibility

Self-hosted Logfire instances expose APIs as they are released, so a feature the provider calls may not exist on an older instance. The minimum Logfire release for each feature is listed below; when a call hits a missing API, the provider says which release it requires.

| Feature | Minimum Logfire release | Minimum Helm chart |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Projects, alerts, dashboards, channels, write/read tokens | All supported releases | - |
| `logfire_api_key`, `logfire_gateway_api_key` | v2026-06-09.01 | - |
| `logfire_organization` create and list, organization import | v2026-06-03.01 | - |
| `logfire_organization` read, update, delete (including setting `billing_email` at creation) | v2026-06-25.01 | - |
| `logfire_gateway_provider` | v2026-08-12.01 | logfire-0.13.40 or newer |
| Delegating project-bound scopes (`project:gateway_proxy`, `project:read_otlp`, `project:write_otlp`) from an organization-wide key | v2026-09-14.01 | logfire-0.13.46 or newer |
| `logfire_schedule`, and `alerts` on `logfire_slo` | The first release after v2026-09-22.02 (not released yet) | - |

The instance version appears in the Logfire UI and in the `Logfire-Version` response header on releases that report a release tag; builds that report only an image identity are treated as unknown. Charts from logfire-0.13.45 report the release tag the chart was built from.

## Required scopes

Each resource states the scopes its calls need. The summary below is the single place to build a provider credential from; the authoritative per-route list is your instance's API documentation at `<base_url>/api/docs` (each operation shows its OAuth scopes).

| Resource | Scopes on the provider credential | Credential shape |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `logfire_project` | `organization:create_project` to create; `project:read` to read; `project:write` to update or delete | An organization-wide key in the project's organization to create; an organization-wide or the project's own key to read and write |
| `logfire_channel` | `organization:read_channel` / `organization:write_channel` | Organization-wide key |
| `logfire_alert`, `logfire_slo` | `project:read_alert` / `project:write_alert` | A key that can reach the project (see below) |
| `logfire_schedule` | `organization:read_channel` / `organization:write_channel` | Organization-wide key |
| `logfire_dashboard` | `project:read_dashboard` / `project:write_dashboard` | A key that can reach the project |
| `logfire_read_token` | `project:read_token` | A key that can reach the project |
| `logfire_write_token` | `project:write_token` | A key that can reach the project |
| `logfire_frontend_application`, `logfire_frontend_application_token` | `project:write_token` / `project:read_token` | A key that can reach the project |
| `logfire_api_key` | `organization:create_api_key` to create; `organization:read_api_key` / `organization:write_api_key` to list, update, or delete; plus every scope the new key is granted | Organization-wide for organization and project scopes; a project-bound scope (`project:read_otlp`, `project:write_otlp`, `project:gateway_proxy`) on an organization-wide key needs `organization:create_api_key` alongside it (delegation only, v2026-09-14.01+) |
| `logfire_gateway_api_key` | `organization:create_api_key`, `organization:read_api_key`, `organization:write_api_key`, and `project:gateway_proxy` | An organization-wide key holding `organization:create_api_key` and `project:gateway_proxy` (delegation only, v2026-09-14.01+), or a project-scoped key for the project |
| `logfire_gateway_provider` | `organization:read` / `organization:write` | Organization-wide key |
| `logfire_organization` | `organization:admin` | A key created in the admin-panel organization (self-hosted only) |

Two rules decide what a credential can do:

- **A key can only delegate scopes it holds**, with one exception on Logfire v2026-09-14.01+ — an organization-wide key that also holds `organization:create_api_key` may hold project-bound scopes purely to delegate them. It cannot exercise them itself; it can pass them to the keys it creates.
- **A key reaches a project when it is organization-wide in the project's organization, or project-scoped to that exact project.** A project-scoped key cannot read or manage any other project, even in the same organization.

### Self-hosted bootstrap

The first credential comes from the admin panel; every later one is minted from a key that already holds the scopes it grants:

1. In the admin organization (the one with the admin panel), create an organization-wide key carrying `organization:admin` to manage organizations with `logfire_organization`.
2. In the organization that owns the projects, create an organization-wide key carrying `organization:create_project`, plus `organization:create_api_key` if Terraform will mint other keys. `logfire_project` creates projects in this key's organization.
3. For the AI Gateway, use either credential shape under "Creating gateway keys" below. `logfire_gateway_provider` additionally needs `organization:read` / `organization:write`, and every `logfire_gateway_api_key` sets `project_id`.

### Creating gateway keys

`project:gateway_proxy` is a project-bound scope: a key can only exercise it when it is bound to a project, which is what gives the gateway a project to resolve providers, budgets, and spend against.

On Logfire v2026-09-14.01 or newer, an organization-wide key that also holds `organization:create_api_key` may carry the scope to delegate it. The scope is delegation-only on that key: the key cannot call the gateway itself (it has no project to resolve), but it can create gateway keys for any project in the organization, so one provider credential covers the project, the gateway provider, and the gateway keys:

```terraform
provider "logfire" {
  # An organization-wide key carrying organization:create_api_key,
  # project:gateway_proxy, and the organization scopes used for projects
  # and gateway providers.
  api_key = var.organization_api_key
}

resource "logfire_project" "example" {
  name = "example"
}

resource "logfire_gateway_api_key" "example" {
  name                 = "example-gateway"
  project_id           = logfire_project.example.id
  spending_limit_daily = 25
}
```

If you would rather the credential that manages gateway keys reach only one project, use a project-scoped operator key and a second provider alias for the `logfire_gateway_api_key` resources. Mint it with `organization:create_api_key`, `organization:read_api_key`, `organization:write_api_key`, and `project:gateway_proxy`, from the **organization-level** API keys page with the project selected: the project's own API keys page rejects the organization-level scopes the key also needs, and without `organization:create_api_key` it cannot create anything. On releases before v2026-09-14.01 this is the only shape that works.

```terraform
provider "logfire" {
  # Creates the project with the organization-wide key.
  api_key = var.organization_api_key
}

provider "logfire" {
  alias = "project"
  # A project-scoped key for that project, holding organization:create_api_key
  # and project:gateway_proxy.
  api_key = var.project_scoped_api_key
}

resource "logfire_project" "example" {
  name = "example"
}

resource "logfire_gateway_api_key" "example" {
  provider             = logfire.project
  name                 = "example-gateway"
  project_id           = logfire_project.example.id
  spending_limit_daily = 25
}
```

## Troubleshooting credentials

- `403 This token is missing the required scope: X.` — the provider credential lacks a scope the call needs. Add `X` to the key and retry.
- `403 You do not have permission to access this project` — the credential cannot reach the project it was given. The key is either project-scoped to a different project, created in a different organization than the project, or the project no longer exists in the key's organization. The API answers the same way for all three on purpose, so it cannot be used to probe.
- `403 The ... scope is not granted to the calling credential and cannot be delegated` — the provider credential was asked to mint a key with a scope it does not hold. For `project:read_otlp`, `project:write_otlp`, and `project:gateway_proxy`, an organization-wide key may delegate them only if it also holds `organization:create_api_key` and the instance runs Logfire v2026-09-14.01 or newer; before that, or for a project-scoped operator, see "Creating gateway keys" above.
- `404 Not Found` from a self-hosted instance — the instance may predate the API being called. See "Self-hosted compatibility" above.

